module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.private_tree_inside_a_public_worktree_publishes_on_archive

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data private_tree_inside_a_public_worktree_publishes_on_archive: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "private_tree_inside_a_public_worktree_publishes_on_archive" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**A PRIVATE REPOSITORY PLACED INSIDE A PUBLIC REPOSITORY'S WORKTREE IS PUBLISHED BY ARCHIVE-TIME AUTO-PUSH, AND THE SESSION THAT PLACED IT IS NOT ALIVE TO PREVENT IT** (INVALID STATE: private content -- a clone, or an unversioned overlay COPY of a private tree -- sits under a worktree whose remote is public, and the harness's archive-time auto-push commits and pushes worktree state without asking. The author is not consulted because the author no longer exists: the session is archived first and the push happens afterwards. THE ROOT IS A RECIPE THE LANES WERE FOLLOWING, NOT AN IMPROVISATION, WHICH IS WHY IT RECURRED ACROSS UNRELATED LANES: the private-witness step of the generated CI workflow copies the private tree into the public checkout -- cp -a ../dag <name>_overlay/dag -- and that step is SAFE IN CI, where the checkout is thrown away, and UNSAFE IN A SESSION, where the worktree persists and is auto-committed. The same instruction is correct in one execution context and a disclosure in the other, and nothing in the instruction distinguishes them. MEASURED INSTANCE, 2026-09-11: session proud-hawk-390 archived at 00:35Z; at 11:15:45Z -- ELEVEN HOURS LATER -- branch session/proud-hawk-390 received a single WIP commit aba7c528 carrying 85,967 lines, base_overlay/ and private_overlay/ copies of the private repository including its finance models and its whole strategy tree. The dashboard then auto-opened a pull request against the PUBLIC repository and a reviewer reviewed the private planning document in public before any human noticed. THE DISTINGUISHING FACT, AND IT IS THE WHOLE ROW: a nested CLONE of the private repository (its own .git, at a path the public repo's .git/info/exclude names) is reported ignored and is NOT an auto-push candidate; an overlay COPY (no .git, at a path nothing excludes) IS. Five worktrees on one host carried private material that night and the two that leaked were the two that were copies at unexcluded paths -- same hazard, same session shape, opposite outcome, decided entirely by the directory name. So the protection is PATH-SHAPED while the hazard is CONTENT-SHAPED, and nothing joins them. AND THE PATH IS NOT EVEN STABLE: the overlay directory name is a PARAMETER of the recipe, so the three observed instances used three different names -- base_overlay, private_overlay, wb62_overlay -- which is why a scan written against the first two names missed the third. A survey keyed on a name is unsound against a producer that takes the name as an argument. WHY THE EXISTING PROTECTION IS A SILENT PRECONDITION RATHER THAN A GUARD: the exclusion lives in .git/info/exclude, which is NOT VERSION CONTROLLED, so it is absent in a fresh clone and in any worktree whose common git dir differs, and its absence is invisible until the push has already happened. A setup that is safe today is unsafe tomorrow for a reason no diff records. CONTAINMENT IS INCOMPLETE BY CONSTRUCTION: closing the pull request and deleting the branch leaves the commit fetchable at refs/pull/<n>/head, which GitHub retains, so the exposure survives every action available from inside the repository and its removal requires the forge operator. THREE THINGS THE FIRST CONTAINMENT MISSED AND SUCCESSIVE SWEEPS FOUND, EACH WIDENING THE POPULATION: a SECOND worktree held the same untracked overlay copy, armed and waiting for its own archive; the archived session's LOCAL branch still held the leak commit and would have re-pushed it on the next harness push; and a SECOND PUBLISHED INSTANCE existed on another branch entirely -- 117 private paths under a third overlay name, committed rather than untracked, so every clean-working-tree check and every name-keyed scan passed over it. It was found only by a widened pattern run against main's own baseline across all remote refs and open pull-request heads. A CLASS THAT RE-ARMS AFTER CONTAINMENT IS NOT CONTAINED UNTIL THE POPULATION IS SWEPT BY IDENTITY AGAINST A BASELINE, and each of the first two sweeps reported completeness it did not have. RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable -- the publication is detected after the fact and the residue is deleted where deletion reaches. CEILING: STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE, and the construction is ordinary rather than clever -- auto-push cannot publish a tree it cannot see, so private material held OUTSIDE the worktree has no constructor for this invalid state at all, and no rule, no exclusion entry and no reviewer is needed to maintain it. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, A CAPABILITY AND NOT AN ARTEFACT, AND CORRECTED ONCE ALREADY BY A SECOND INSTANCE: the private-witness step must materialise its overlay OUTSIDE the public checkout, so that neither a throwaway CI checkout nor a persistent session worktree can contain foreign private content at any moment. THE TRIGGER THIS ROW FIRST CARRIED WAS INSUFFICIENT AND IS RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN QUIETLY REPLACED: it said archive-time auto-push must refuse UNTRACKED trees. The second instance, commit 86df0fd62f on session/warm-badger-62-zero-field-hole, was ALREADY COMMITTED when it was pushed -- git status was clean -- so an untracked-tree refusal would have been satisfied while the capability stayed dead, which is the DESIGN section 4b(3) grain mismatch this ledger exists to catch, committed by this row's own author within hours of policing it elsewhere. WHAT DOES NOT RETIRE THIS ROW, stated because it is the tempting discharge and it is [[a_written_row_is_not_a_firing_mechanism]] exactly: a brief instructing lanes to clone outside the worktree, or to run a path check after cloning. Both are PUSH friction delivered to named authors, both expire when those lanes archive, and neither makes the dangerous form harder to type for the next lane that copies a tree. The verification step is worth sending and is not the capability.) ",

    "**THE ADJACENT ROWS AND WHY NEITHER OWNS THIS CLASS.** [[a_branch_property_falsified_by_a_derived_push]] is about a push INVALIDATING A PROPERTY ASSERTED OF A BRANCH; here no property is falsified -- the push is faithful to the worktree and that is precisely the defect, because the worktree contained something that was never meant to be a publication subject. [[publish_over_a_path_a_reader_is_serving]] is about publication COLLIDING WITH A CONCURRENT READER over one path; here there is no collision and no reader, and the harm is that the publication happened at all, to an audience defined by the remote's visibility rather than by any path. A THIRD BOUNDARY IS OFTEN ASSERTED HERE AND IT IS NOT ON RECORD: auto-push publishing PARTIAL worktree state -- a half-finished change escaping as if finished -- has NO ROW IN THIS DIRECTORY, and this receipt originally claimed it did. The claim came from an operator-session memory rather than from the corpus, which is a citation to something no reader can reach, in the one receipt whose whole job is to establish that no existing class owns this one. It is recorded rather than deleted because the near-miss is the useful part: an unresolvable boundary reads exactly like a checked one, and the row demonstrated the resolvable form two sentences earlier. The partial-state class remains UNFILED; whoever files it owns that boundary, and until then no reader may treat this row as distinguished from it. THIS row is about state that is COMPLETE, CORRECT, AND FOREIGN: the bytes are exactly right and belong to a different repository with a different visibility, so no completeness check and no staging discipline reaches it. The composition -- a private repository nested inside a public one -- is what is new, and it is what makes an ordinary archive into a disclosure. ",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
